<h2>Signs Your System Is Ready for an LED Upgrade</h2>
<p>Halogen landscape lighting was the standard in Austin through the 2000s and plenty of it is still running. You can usually tell: a few fixtures are dark, the ones that work are slightly different colors, the transformer hums and runs warm, and somebody has to crawl around the beds every spring with a bag of bulbs. A 20-fixture halogen system draws around 700 watts every night; the equivalent LED system draws 100 to 140. LED landscape lighting Austin homeowners switch to today also runs cooler, holds a consistent color and goes 40,000 hours or more between lamp changes.</p>
<p>Austin Landscape Lighting has converted hundreds of older systems since 2020. The work is less dramatic than a new installation and often more satisfying, because the design is already there and the retrofit simply makes it work the way it was meant to.</p>

<h2>Two Paths to LED: Retrofit or Replace</h2>
<h3>Lamp retrofit</h3>
<p>If your fixtures are solid brass or copper and the sockets are clean, we keep them and install LED lamps in the same MR16, bi-pin or wedge base. Good LED lamps for landscape use are rated for the heat inside a sealed fixture, available in 2700K with high color rendering and offered in the same beam spreads as the halogens they replace. The fixture looks identical; only the light and the electric bill change.</p>
<h3>Fixture replacement</h3>
<p>Aluminum and plastic fixtures that have corroded, cracked or lost their lenses are not worth re-lamping. We replace them with brass integrated-LED or lamp-ready fixtures on the existing cable runs. Integrated fixtures seal the LED and driver inside a weatherproof body, which is the most reliable option for Austin's heat and irrigation exposure. Most retrofits end up a mix: keep the sound brass, replace the failed aluminum.</p>
<h3>The transformer question</h3>
<p>This is the step most do-it-yourself conversions miss. Old magnetic transformers were sized for halogen loads and may now be running at 10 or 15 percent of capacity, which can cause flicker with some LED drivers. Multi-tap transformers that were set to 13 or 14 volts to overcome halogen voltage drop may now overdrive LEDs. We measure the actual load after conversion, reset taps so every fixture sees 11 to 12 volts, and replace transformers that are undersized, oversized or lacking a photocell and timer. The <a href="/tools/transformer-calculator/">transformer calculator</a> shows how a lower LED load changes sizing. Many clients take the opportunity to add <a href="/services/smart-lighting-systems/">smart control</a> at the same time, since the transformer is being handled anyway.</p>

<h2>What the Savings Look Like</h2>
<table>
