<tr><td>35 fixtures</td><td>1,225 W</td><td>210 W</td><td>2,683 kWh vs 460 kWh</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Those figures assume 35-watt halogens replaced with 6-watt LEDs and are planning estimates, not measurements of your system. Add the cost of halogen bulbs replaced every 2,000 to 4,000 hours, which is roughly every year to eighteen months at six hours a night, and the lamp savings alone usually cover the retrofit within a few years. Our <a href="/tools/led-savings-calculator/">LED savings calculator</a> runs the numbers for your fixture count and electric rate.</p>

<h2>Getting the Light Right, Not Just Efficient</h2>
<p>An LED retrofit is a chance to fix the design flaws that crept in over the years. We re-aim every fixture, replace mismatched lamps so the whole property is a consistent 2700K, swap beam spreads where a narrow spot ended up on a wide wall, and add glare shields where fixtures have been exposed by plant growth or removal. Lumen output is matched to purpose: 200 to 450 for architectural uplights, 400 to 900 for large trees, 100 to 200 for path lights. Brighter is not the goal; even and warm is.</p>
<p>Connections get attention too. Halogen-era systems were often wired with pierce-point clips that corrode in Austin's caliche soil and irrigation moisture. Dark fixtures are frequently a connection problem rather than a lamp problem, so we remake every connection we touch with waterproof gel-filled connectors.</p>

<h2>Austin-Specific Notes</h2>
<p><strong>Heat tolerance.</strong> Not every LED lamp survives inside a sealed brass fixture in a bed that hits 140 degrees in July. We specify lamps and integrated fixtures rated for high ambient temperature, which is the difference between a 15-year LED and one that fails in its second summer.</p>
<p><strong>Color consistency.</strong> Austin limestone and live oak bark look best under 2700K with a high color rendering index. Mixing warehouse-store 3000K and 5000K lamps into an existing system is the most common mistake we are called to correct.</p>
<p><strong>Dark-sky friendly by default.</strong> LED lamps let us hit the right light level at lower output than halogen ever could, and better optics keep the beam on the target. Combined with 2700K color and shielding, a retrofitted system is typically far more dark-sky friendly than the halogen system it replaces, which matters in Westlake Hills, Lakeway and Dripping Springs.</p>

<h2>Process and Warranty</h2>
<p>We start with a free on-site evaluation: fixture count and condition, transformer type and load, cable and connection condition, and a night look at what is working. Your written estimate separates lamp retrofits, fixture replacements and transformer work so you can see exactly where the cost sits. Licensed and insured technicians complete most retrofits in one visit, followed by a night aiming session. Austin Landscape Lighting warrants the workmanship for 2 years, and the LED lamps and fixtures carry manufacturer warranties, typically 5 years or longer on the brass fixtures we install. Planning ranges above are planning figures, not quotes.</p>

